Abstraction-
	Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and showing only the essential functionality to the user. It allows users to know what an object does without knowing how it does it. In Java, abstraction is achieved using abstract classes and interfaces.
	
Real-Life Analogy - Car - you use steering wheel but don't need to know how fuel is injected and how the engine combusts fuel. You only use the functionality this is abstraction.

Example - 
	how to use it - 
		abstract class Vehicle {
		    abstract void start();
		}
		
	how to implement it - 
		class Car extends Vehicle {

		    @Override
		    void start() {
			System.out.println("Car starts using a key or push button.");
		    }
		}

		class Bike extends Vehicle {

		    @Override
		    void start() {
			System.out.println("Bike starts using a self-start or kick.");
		    }
		}

	Usage - 
		public class Main {

		    public static void main(String[] args) {

			Vehicle v1 = new Car();
			Vehicle v2 = new Bike();

			v1.start();
			v2.start();
		    }
		}
		
Another example using Interface - 
		interface Payment {
		    void pay(double amount);
		}

	Implementation - 
		class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {

		    @Override
		    public void pay(double amount) {
			System.out.println("Paid using Credit Card");
		    }
		}

		class UpiPayment implements Payment {

		    @Override
		    public void pay(double amount) {
			System.out.println("Paid using UPI");
		    }
		}
		
	Usage - 
		Payment payment = new UpiPayment();

		payment.pay(500);
		
Why Do We Need Abstraction?
	Without abstraction:
		The user would need to know every implementation detail.
	With abstraction:
		The user only knows: pay(). and not which bank or which api to call or what algorithm.
		
		
How is Abstraction Achieved in Java?
	1. Abstract Class
		Can have both abstract and concrete methods.
		Can have constructors and instance variables.
		A class can extend only one abstract class.
	2. Interface
		Defines a contract that implementing classes must follow.
		A class can implement multiple interfaces.
		
Abstraction is the process of hiding implementation details and exposing only the essential functionality to the user. It helps reduce complexity by allowing users to focus on what an object does rather than how it does it. In Java, abstraction is achieved using abstract classes and interfaces.


Abstract Class vs Interface -
	An abstract class is used when multiple related classes share common state (fields) and behavior (implemented methods). It can have both abstract and concrete methods.

	An interface defines a contract that classes agree to implement. It is used to specify what a class should do, without forcing a shared implementation. A class can implement multiple interfaces, enabling multiple inheritance of type.
	
